  "privacyPolicyCurrentContent": "Last updated: 2026-10-02\n\n**At a glance**\n- Files and organization data are handled primarily on your device.\n- When you choose to share, export, or send data for support, the selected data leaves the App.\n- Charms and notes are normally stored on this device. Removing the app deletes those records together with the in-app copies.\n\n## 1. Policy and operator\nCharmShelf (the \"App\") uses a local-first design that handles data primarily on your device. The App does not automatically upload library data to a developer-operated server.\n\n- Operator: Private Shelf Project (CharmShelf Development Team)\n- Contact: [deeple+charmshelfstaff@inkboxmail.com](mailto:deeple+charmshelfstaff@inkboxmail.com)\n\n## 2. Information handled by the App\n- App-managed copies of files you import and external files you authorize the App to reference\n- Folder structure, charms, aliases, notes, favorites, tiers, sort order, hidden state, and archive state\n- Display preferences, thumbnails, cache, and diagnostic or crash logs generated on the device\n- Product details and prices, transaction identifiers and status, and information needed to finish optional support purchases\n\n- Android import sources: names, locations, types, sizes, modification times and identities are read from locations you select and authorize through the system picker to display import candidates. No new persistent index or in-place organization data is created. Indexes, charms, catalog names and favorites saved by older versions remain in private app storage for migration. Original files are not modified.\n\n## 3. External transfer, backup, and tracking\nThe App does not include advertising SDKs or behavioral analytics SDKs.\n\n- When you choose to share, export, or send data for support, the selected data goes to the destination you choose.\n- Product queries, purchases, and completion of support transactions exchange information with the OS store. The App also communicates with the store at startup and when returning to the foreground to check and finish pending transactions.\n- Charm definitions can be exported as JSON. This does not back up the entire library, including assignments to works, favorites, and notes.\n- Downloading works on Android also exports associated organization details, such as charm assignments, notes, names, and storage locations, to the same destination. Sharing that destination with another app or service also shares these details.\n- App-managed private storage is configured to be excluded from automatic cloud backup and device-to-device transfer.\n- Android public recovery folders, external source files, and data held by external services are outside that exclusion. Data you share, export, or send for support may also be moved or copied depending on the destination and device settings.\n\n## 4. Why permissions are used\n- Storage and file access: select, reference, copy, organize, display, export, and restore files\n- Photo library: import or export photos and videos you select\n- Biometrics: unlock the App. The OS processes biometric data; the App does not obtain or store the biometric data itself\n- Notifications: show file import progress and completion. Imports remain available without notification permission\n\n- Android file selection: the system file and folder pickers limit access to locations you authorize. Read access for registered import sources is retained on the device for reuse. Removing a source does not delete original files. The App does not request all files access.\n\n## 5. Limits of protection\nHidden and archived states organize visibility inside the App; they do not encrypt the underlying files. App Lock restricts access to the App screen but cannot fully prevent device-level or OS-level access.\n\n- Organization data and backup: Files and organization data are stored on this device. Settings offers Library Backup for app-managed files, charms, notes, favorites, and viewing history. Hidden, archived, and trashed content is included; backups are not encrypted. Choose a destination you control and store the backup securely. Restoration adds a dated dedicated folder and preserves existing data with the same identity. Copy legacy external references into the library first. Passcodes, biometric settings, and OS access permissions are excluded. Exporting only charm definitions does not back up the library.\n- Original files: Keep separate copies outside the App. Check that they open before deleting the App or resetting the device.\n\n## 6. Retention and deletion\nDeletion effects depend on where data is stored.\n\n- Removing an external reference normally removes only its management record.\n- Private app storage and organization data saved inside the App are normally removed when the App is uninstalled.\n- Android public recovery folders, referenced source files, shared destinations, and exported files may remain.\n- Charms and catalog settings are not automatically restored from external original files.\n\n- Device-file indexes and organization details saved by older versions remain on the device after access is revoked. Migration, Clear All Data and uninstalling affect the scope explained on screen. Removing an import source does not delete original files.\n\n## 7. Use by minors\nThe App does not provide a feature that collects children's or minors' personal data on a developer-operated server. Minors should use the App with guardian consent and supervision where required.\n\n## 8. Updates and contact\nThis policy may be updated when features or applicable laws change. Contact the address above with privacy questions.",
